.env
searxng/settings.generated.yml
logs/
open-webui-data/
backup/
*.pyc
.claude/docs

# Local machine config (paths/hosts) — see config.example.sh
config.sh
.config/
# Run logs + data artifacts (revisit-able locally; consider blob upload later)
eval_results/
memtest_prefill_exercise/
benchmark/*.log
benchmark/bfcl_runs/
# Operator control files for bench.workqueue (STOP/SKIP/PAUSE) — transient runtime state, not plan
# config; the queue file itself stays committed. Default location when $STACK_WORKDIR is unset.
benchmark/control/
# Per-item bench_watch.py output + the aggregated current.md — transient runtime state, not plan
# config. Default location when $STACK_WORKDIR is unset (see bench/workqueue.py::_status_dir).
benchmark/status/

# benchmark/results/ is DELIBERATELY TRACKED (operator instruction 2026-08-14): "we need to record
# these in the repo so that we have history to compare".
#
# WHY IT USED TO BE IGNORED, AND WHY THAT WAS THE WRONG CALL. Ignoring it meant results existed on
# exactly one box with no history, which cost us twice: (1) the retired M2 Max took its entire corpus
# with it, and those rows are NOT re-measurable at any price; (2) `--clean-stale` DELETES rows whose
# provenance fingerprint no longer matches, and with results untracked there was no way back. Tracking
# them also makes the corpus visible from the DRIVER box, which is what the scoresheet generator needs
# (before this, `scoreboard.py` on the driver reported NOT MEASURED for every dimension because the rows
# were all on the worker).
#
# COST, measured 2026-08-14 rather than feared: 16 MB total — 14 MB across 93 append-only .jsonl, and
# the largest single file is 7.2 MB (ifeval). Rows carry NO dataset prompt (verified: the schema has
# `content`/`answer_gold` but no `prompt`), so tracking them publishes our own completions, not
# benchmark prompts. jsonl appends diff and pack well.
#
# DISCIPLINE: rows are written by the WORKER only, so result commits originate there and the driver
# only ever reads them — that keeps this from becoming a merge-conflict surface.
benchmark/results/**/__pycache__/
# ...EXCEPT the top-level aggregates. `scores.json` holds only the pairs of the LAST grade_all call, so
# it differs between boxes by construction and would be a permanent merge-conflict surface. The durable
# per-pair record is results/<model>/<bench>.score.json, which IS tracked. (scoreboard.py refuses to read
# scores.json for the same reason: grading one model erases every other model's entry.)
benchmark/results/scores*.json

# bfcl_eval drops empty runtime lock files inside its result tree; zero information, never commit.
benchmark/results/**/.file_locks/
